The notes typically show, for each class of intangible:

- the measurement basis (usually cost);
- amortization methods and useful lives;
- opening and closing cost and accumulated amortization, with additions, disposals and impairment losses;
- whether the assets were internally generated;
- whether lives are finite or indefinite.

Analysts care because accounting choices change the numbers. Expensing R&D lowers current profit and assets. If the firm is growing, this builds a hidden reserve, since the spending is charged now while the related sales come later. A firm that capitalizes development costs shows higher profit and higher assets in the year of spending, and the cash outflow appears in investing rather than operating cash flow. When comparing companies, adjust for these differences, and remember that big balance sheet intangibles come mostly from *purchased* items, because home-grown ones are mostly off the balance sheet.
